# Grace Hopper

Grace Brewster Murray Hopper (1906–1992) was an American computer scientist, mathematician, and United States Navy rear admiral who pioneered the development of accessible computer programming languages. One of the first programmers of the Harvard Mark I computer during World War II, she was instrumental in shaping the foundational concepts of software engineering and machine-independent programming. Hopper firmly believed that computer code should be written in a language close to natural English rather than machine code, a radical concept that directly led to her creation of the first compiler, the A-0 System, in 1952. Her subsequent work and advocacy laid the structural framework for COBOL (Common Business-Oriented Language), one of the earliest and most widely used high-level programming languages. Throughout her extensive military and civilian career, she served on the teams developing the UNIVAC I, the first known large-scale commercial computer, and helped standardize military network protocols. Hopper's insistence on standardizing testing systems for computer languages heavily influenced both the United States Department of Defense and the broader commercial software industry. For her profound impact on computing architecture and naval technology, she was awarded the National Medal of Technology in 1991 and posthumously received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2016.

## Early Life and Education

Grace Brewster Murray was born on December 9, 1906, in New York City. She was the eldest of three children in a family that strongly valued education. Her father, Walter Fletcher Murray, was an insurance broker, and her mother, Mary Campbell Van Horne, shared a strong interest in mathematics. Demonstrating an early mechanical curiosity, a seven-year-old Hopper famously dismantled seven alarm clocks to determine how they worked before her mother intervened.

Hopper attended the Hartridge School in Plainfield, New Jersey, before entering Vassar College in 1924. She graduated in 1928 with a bachelor's degree in mathematics and physics. She subsequently enrolled at Yale University, where she earned a master's degree in mathematics in 1930. In 1934, she became one of the first women to earn a Ph.D. in mathematics from Yale; her dissertation, supervised by Norwegian mathematician Øystein Ore, was titled "New Types of Irreducibility Criteria." During her graduate studies and afterward, she taught mathematics at Vassar, eventually achieving the rank of associate professor in 1941.

## World War II and the Harvard Mark I

Following the United States' entry into World War II, Hopper attempted to enlist in the military but was initially rejected because, at age 34, she was deemed too old, and her weight-to-height ratio was below the military standard. Furthermore, her profession as a mathematician and professor was considered crucial to the war effort on the home front. However, persistent in her goal, she obtained a waiver and was sworn into the United States Navy Reserve Women's Reserve (known as the WAVES) in December 1943.

Commissioned as a lieutenant (junior grade), Hopper was assigned to the Bureau of Ships Computation Project at Harvard University in 1944. There, she served under Howard H. Aiken and became one of the first programmers of the IBM Automatic Sequence Controlled Calculator, better known as the Harvard Mark I. This massive electromechanical computer was used to calculate rocket trajectories, create range tables for anti-aircraft guns, and calibrate minesweepers. Hopper co-authored a 500-page manual detailing the operating principles of the Mark I, which stood as one of the first comprehensive texts on computer programming.

While working on the subsequent Mark II computer in 1947, Hopper and her team discovered a moth trapped in a relay, which was causing the machine to malfunction. While the term "bug" had been used by engineers like Thomas Edison for decades to describe mechanical flaws, Hopper and her team were the first to physically tape a moth into a logbook with the notation, "First actual case of bug being found," thereby popularizing the terms "bug" and "debugging" in the context of computer science.

## Compilers and the UNIVAC I

In 1949, Hopper declined a full professorship at Vassar College in favor of remaining a reservist and joining the Eckert-Mauchly Computer Corporation as a senior mathematician. The company, founded by ENIAC creators J. Presper Eckert and John Mauchly, was developing the UNIVAC I (Universal Automatic Computer I).

During her time at Eckert-Mauchly (which was later acquired by Remington Rand), Hopper advanced her theory that computer programs should be written in a language that humans could easily read and understand. Management initially dismissed the idea, arguing that computers could only understand pure arithmetic. Undeterred, Hopper developed the A-0 System in 1952. The A-0 was the first known compiler—a program that translates mathematical code into machine-readable binary code.

By 1954, Hopper was named the company's director of automatic programming. Her department released some of the first compiler-based programming languages, including MATH-MATIC and FLOW-MATIC. FLOW-MATIC was particularly revolutionary as it was the first programming language to use English words for operations (such as "ADD" or "COMPARE") rather than mathematical symbols.

## The Development of COBOL

Hopper's work on FLOW-MATIC proved that computers could be programmed using an English-like syntax, which paved the way for a universal, machine-independent business language. In 1959, computer experts from industry and government gathered at the Conference on Data Systems Languages (CODASYL) to create a standard language for business data processing.

Hopper served as a key technical consultant to the CODASYL committee. The resulting language, COBOL (Common Business-Oriented Language), was heavily based on the design principles of Hopper's FLOW-MATIC and IBM's COMTRAN. While she did not single-handedly invent COBOL, her pioneering concepts formed its foundation, and she aggressively championed its adoption. She utilized her influence within the Navy to mandate the use of COBOL in military systems, which effectively forced hardware manufacturers to support the language, cementing its status as the dominant business programming language of the 20th century.

## Later Naval Career and Standardization

Hopper retired from the Naval Reserve in 1966 with the rank of commander. However, she was recalled to active duty in August 1967 for what was intended to be a six-month assignment. Her task was to standardize the Navy's disparate high-level computer languages and develop validation software for COBOL and its compiler.

This assignment ultimately lasted nearly two decades. She served as the director of the Navy Programming Languages Group within the Navy's Office of Information Systems Planning. In this capacity, she developed validation software for computer systems, creating rigorous testing standards that were subsequently adopted by the National Bureau of Standards (now the National Institute of Standards and Technology).

By special presidential appointment, she was promoted to the rank of captain in 1973. In 1983, a joint resolution of Congress promoted her to the rank of commodore, a title which was renamed rear admiral (lower half) in 1985. Hopper retired involuntarily from the Navy on August 14, 1986. At nearly 80 years old, she was the oldest active-duty commissioned officer in the United States Navy at the time of her retirement.

## Later Life and Legacy

Following her military retirement, Hopper was hired as a senior consultant to the Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC), a position she held until her death. She traveled extensively, giving hundreds of lectures per year on the early days of computing and the importance of adapting to technological change.

Grace Hopper died on January 1, 1992, in Arlington, Virginia, and was interred with full military honors at Arlington National Cemetery. Her legacy is deeply embedded in the structure of modern software engineering. The United States Navy Arleigh Burke-class guided-missile destroyer USS *Hopper* (DDG-70) is named in her honor, as is the Cray XE6 "Hopper" supercomputer at the National Energy Research Scientific Computing Center. Additionally, the Grace Hopper Celebration of Women in Computing, the world's largest gathering of women technologists, was established in 1994 to honor her contributions and encourage female participation in computer science.

## Works or Publications

* "A Manual of Operation for the Automatic Sequence Controlled Calculator" (1946) — Co-authored with Howard H. Aiken.
* "The Education of a Computer" (1952) — Proceedings of the ACM National Meeting.
* "Understanding Computers" (1984) — Lecture and subsequent publication widely outlining her philosophy on technology management.

